{
  "id": "error-handlers",
  "title": "Error handlers",
  "group": "Yon server",
  "summary": "An error handler turns a typed error into a public answer, in one place for every route, as Spring's `@ControllerAdvice` does (ADR 0051). Put `ErrorHandler` on a class in `server/controllers/` whose name ends with `ErrorHandler`, and `HandlesError` on each method that answers an error. `HandlesError` takes no argument: the type of the method's first parameter says which error it handles, and an optional second parameter receives the request in the language's own type. An error type lives in `server/errors/` and has no annotation. `ResponseStatus` sets a status from 400 to 599, and 500 when it is left out. Tachyon checks the answer against its model, and OPTIONS and `/api.json` list each handled error with its status and shape. The nearest type wins: a handler for a subclass runs before a handler for its base class. Go matches with `errors.As`, so a wrapped error matches too, and Rust matches the error type of the controller method's `Result`. Two methods that handle the same error type stop the build, and so do the Spring names `ControllerAdvice` and `ExceptionHandler`. An error that no method handles, or an error thrown inside a handler, keeps the fixed 500 answer; the one exception is the error of a `Check` parameter, which Yon answers with 422 and the broken rules (ADR 0101). Nothing from the error reaches the client unless the handler returns it. The order is the controller, then the error handler, then middleware `after`. Error handlers are Yon only, and every Yon language has them. `ty add feature error-handlers` adds a JavaScript or TypeScript example with its tests.",
